  "title": {
    "en": "Larks and buntings"
  },
  "subtitle": {
    "en": "Skylark, Woodlark, Shore Lark, Snow and Lapland Bunting, Corn, Cirl and Reed Bunting"
  },
  "intro": {
    "en": "Larks and buntings are the small brown birds of fields, dunes and reedbeds. Larks walk, buntings hop, and both give calls that identify them. They are easy to overlook, and winter is the best time to see them, because flocks gather on stubble fields, saltmarshes and the coast.\n\nThe rarities of today live on the beach: Shore Larks, Snow Buntings and Lapland Buntings are Arctic birds that winter on the coast of the North Sea. The Woodlark and the Cirl Bunting are southern birds in the UK and France, and the Corn Bunting is declining over most of the region.\n\nLearn them by call: the Skylark's liquid trill, the Corn Bunting's jingling keys, the Reed Bunting's single high note."
  },
  "words": [
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "song flight"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A flight in which the bird sings while hovering or circling high. Skylarks and Woodlarks do it."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "crest"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A short tuft on the head that can be raised. The Skylark has a small one."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "wing patch"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "A white patch on the wing, as on the Snow Bunting."
      }
    },
    {
      "term": {
        "en": "tail pattern"
      },
      "explain": {
        "en": "The pattern of white on the tail. Larks and buntings often show white outer tail feathers."
      }
    }
  ],
